<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e6f2f8; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 1 – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-style: italic; margin-top: 5px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 75%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> The Importance of Water</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Clean Water Is Important Because...</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Comprehend multi-step instructions for academic tasks. Interactive: Respond to questions and requests in a school setting.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can follow multi-step instructions about water usage in a school setting (e.g., "Please turn off the tap.")<br>Can respond to questions about water conservation (e.g., "Why is it important to save water?" "If we don't save water, we will face water shortages.")<br>Can identify details from a spoken explanation of the importance of water (e.g., "What do we use water for?" "For cooking, drinking, and agriculture.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e6f2f8; color: #1a5276; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e6f2f8; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e6f2f8; color: #1a5276; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification of key concepts and vocabulary.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows pictures of different uses of water (e.g., drinking, cooking, washing hands, watering plants, agriculture). The teacher asks students, "What do you see?" and "What is water used for?" The teacher plays a short audio clip of running water or rain and asks, "What do you hear?" "What are we talking about today?" (Expected answer: Water). This activates prior knowledge.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher introduces 8-10 core vocabulary words related to the theme using flashcards or images (e.g., <i>water, tap, save, waste, shortage, potable, resource, important, filter, drought</i>). The teacher clearly pronounces each word, emphasizing stress patterns for compound words like "important" and "forecast" (if included). The teacher provides simple definitions or synonyms for each word. For example, "Potable water means water that is safe to drink." The teacher models the first conditional sentence: "If we waste water, there will be less for everyone."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the new vocabulary and concepts.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'potable' water clean or dirty?" (Expected answer: Clean.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If we 'save' water, do we use a lot or a little?" (Expected answer: A little.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is a 'shortage' of water?" (Expected answer: Not enough water.)</li>
                </ul>
                The teacher also asks about the first conditional: "If it rains, will the plants be dry or wet?" (Expected answer: Wet.)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "The Importance of Water."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher plays a short audio explanation (2-3 minutes) about the importance of water and how we use it daily, incorporating the target vocabulary (e.g., <i>potable, resource, save, tap</i>) and grammatical structures (e.g., "We use water for drinking, cooking, and cleaning," "If we don't save water, we will face water shortages"). The audio could be a simple public service announcement or a short educational clip. Genre: "Educational audio explanation/announcement." Register: Informative. Format: Audio recording.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> To comprehend information and instructions in short spoken messages related to daily life and academic tasks.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students listen to the audio twice.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question (First listening):</b> The teacher asks, "What is the main idea of this audio? Is it about cars or water?" (Expected answer: Water). "What is the audio telling us about water?" (Expected answer: Water is important/We need to save water).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (Second listening):</b> Students are given a worksheet with 3-4 True/False statements based on the audio details. For example:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"We use water for cooking and drinking." (T/F)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If we waste water, there will be more water." (T/F)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Water is a limited resource." (T/F)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Students listen and match spoken phrases to pictures (e.g., "turn off the tap" to a picture of a tap being turned off).</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening, integrating grammar and vocabulary.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Listening for specific information, identifying key vocabulary (<i>water, resource, shortage, tap, save, waste, important</i>), and understanding first conditional sentences and expressions of purpose ("for + verb + ing," "because").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled practice (Listening Cloze):</b> The teacher reads sentences aloud slowly. Students listen and complete sentences on a worksheet with the missing target vocabulary words. Example: "We need to ______ water because it's an important ______." (Expected: save, resource). "If we don't save water, we will have a water ______." (Expected: shortage).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled (Listen and Circle):</b> The teacher reads pairs of sentences. Students listen and circle the sentence that correctly uses the target vocabulary or grammar. Example: "a) We use water for drinking. b) We use water drinking." Students circle 'a'. Or, the teacher gives two options orally for a sentence and students identify the correct one. "Listen: 'We must save water' or 'We must waste water'? Circle what you hear."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Listen and Respond):</b> In pairs, students take turns reading short questions and listening to their partner's response. The questions include target vocabulary and grammar. Example: Student A asks, "Why is water important?" Student B responds, "Water is important because we use it for cooking and drinking." Student A asks, "If we don't turn off the tap, what will happen?" Student B responds, "If we don't turn off the tap, we will waste water." They practice listening to questions and formulating appropriate responses.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "The Importance of Water."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task: Listen and Follow Multi-step Instructions for Water Conservation.</b> Students work in small groups (3-4 students). The teacher gives multi-step instructions related to water usage and conservation in a school setting, integrating new vocabulary and grammar. Students must listen carefully and perform the actions or respond verbally as a group or individually. This focuses on receptive listening for academic tasks and interactive listening for responding to requests.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher states instructions such as: "Listen carefully. First, imagine you are at the water fountain. Now, turn off the imaginary tap completely. Second, discuss with your group: If we save water at school, what will happen? Write down one idea. Third, tell me one reason why it's important to have clean water."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen to the instructions and perform the actions (e.g., miming turning off a tap, writing a short phrase, verbally responding).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher may give varied instructions like, "Point to the picture of a 'drought'. What happens in a drought? If there is a drought, will we have a lot of water?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">This task directly addresses the specific objective of comprehending multi-step instructions and responding to questions/requests in a school setting, fostering collaborative interaction and fluency in listening comprehension.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment and student self-assessment.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy: Observation Checklist.</b> The teacher uses an observation checklist during the Performance stage to assess students' listening comprehension and ability to follow instructions and respond appropriately. Criteria include:</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to follow multi-step instructions (e.g., turning off the tap completely, discussing a point).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible responses to questions and requests (e.g., "Why is it important to save water?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (e.g., <i>save, shortage, resource, important</i>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of understanding of cause-effect relationships (e.g., "If we waste water...").</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a short exit ticket with the following prompts:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Write down one new word you learned today about water."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one instruction you heard today about saving water?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How well did you understand the instructions today? (Circle one: Very well / Okay / A little)"</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student and teacher reflection for continuous improvement.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the easiest part of listening today? What was the most challenging part?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What can you do to improve your listening comprehension in English?"</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this lesson? (e.g., The audio explanation engaged students, the multi-step instructions provided a clear context for listening practice).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? (e.g., Some students struggled with the pace of the audio; provide a transcript or simpler audio next time. More repetition of first conditional structures might be beneficial).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Which vocabulary words were most challenging for students? (e.g., 'drought' or 'shortage').</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson focused on listening to introduce vocabulary and concepts about water importance. The next lesson (Lesson 2: Reading Comprehension) will use the same core vocabulary and themes in a written text, allowing students to reinforce understanding and identify the words in a different context, building upon the listening foundation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>